# Security & Configuration

- Never hard-code API keys, secrets, credentials, or tokens in source.
- Secrets come from environment variables or an appropriate secret-management
  mechanism — never from committed files.
- Never commit `.env` files containing real credentials; maintain an
  appropriate `.gitignore` for this.
- When environment configuration needs documenting, provide `.env.example`
  with placeholder values only (e.g. `OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...`).
- Treat all inbound webhook content (e.g. Twilio SMS payloads) as untrusted
  input — validate and sanitize at the transport boundary.
- Before the Twilio SMS integration is considered production-ready, validate
  webhook authenticity using Twilio's recommended request-signature
  verification mechanism.
